{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789868056,"nonce":"KJ2kwIRkVSNI5QmzRBwjL3Mp8zM=","title":"Decision: manual rank for boards and backlogs (needs its own merge semantics)","message":"Raised while reviewing the schema design (6555e36). Two of the five target workflows need manual ordering — the live kanban (f32ea71, drag a card within a column) and sprint planning (cd41e40, order the backlog pool) — and there is no rank in bb9e89e's kind list.\n\nThis is not just a missing kind. Rank needs merge semantics the other kinds don't: under last-writer-wins, two people reordering concurrently means one person's drag silently disappears, which is the exact failure the op-based CRDT exists to prevent. Jira uses LexoRank for this reason.\n\nOptions:\n(a) A `rank` field kind holding a fractional index (a lexicographic key between its neighbours). Inserts never collide, so concurrent drags both survive and produce a deterministic order. Costs a key-generation scheme and periodic rebalancing when keys grow long.\n(b) A plain ordinal number, renumbered on reorder. Simple; loses a concurrent drag, and a reorder touches many issues instead of one.\n(c) No manual rank — order by priority then date. Cheapest, and probably fine until the first in-person kanban session where someone wants to drag a card.\n\nDecide before f32ea71 builds a board on top of whatever is there, and ideally before 5b09ee1 fixes the value encoding. Implementation can wait for the board; the semantics should not.\n\nNote it interacts with the no-multi-entity-commit rule: (b) rewriting N issues' ranks is N commits, eventually consistent, so a reorder would be visibly non-atomic. (a) touches one issue per drag.","files":null},{"type":9,"timestamp":1789868056,"nonce":"Eskfi6ye/sYE6cyWLwehNvrbw40=","key":"type","value":"decision"},{"type":9,"timestamp":1789868056,"nonce":"80Uy2hP6WQ4nwlRp8FvXXhW9HQE=","key":"status","value":"to-do"}]}